Found while building the case matrix for karr #58 (sops 3.13.3). Same family as #58 -- the emitter writes something value_to_bytes did not digest -- but none of these is the 16/17-digit case and none is fixed by fixing #58. Measured with an unencrypted leaf, default unencrypted_suffix:

  format  value   emitted   digest   result
  yaml    -0.0    0         -0       sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL
  yaml    +Inf    Inf       +Inf     sops -d exit 51 (self MAC OK)
  yaml    NaN     NaN       NaN      exit 0 -- works, by luck: Go reads bare NaN as the
                                     STRING NaN, whose ToBytes is also NaN
  json    NaN     null      NaN      sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL
  json    +Inf    null      +Inf     sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL
  json    -Inf    null      -Inf     sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL

JSON -0.0 is NOT in this list: Cpanel writes -0.0, which reparses as the NV -0.0 and digests as -0, so it works. That is exactly what ADR 0005 bought, and any fix here must not lose it -- the naive form of #58 option (a) does lose it (Math::BigFloat->new(-0) drops the sign, exit 51).

For YAML the fix is probably the same dualvar mechanism #58 is considering: with it, +Inf and -Inf emit as +Inf/-Inf and sops -d goes to exit 0 (measured). -0.0 stays broken either way (emitting -0 makes Go yaml.v3 resolve an int 0, digest 0, still a mismatch), so YAML -0.0 may be a genuine no-representation case for a croak -- the argument the int64 range check already makes.

For JSON there is no representation for NaN or Inf at all, so the honest answer is probably to refuse rather than write null and hand the caller a document that fails its own MAC. That is an API/wire policy decision, not a mechanical fix.

Do not fold this into #58. Sequence it after, so the two ADRs stay separable.
RESCOPED after karr #59 landed (commit 4a56b20). Five of the six rows in this ticket's matrix are now closed: assert_representable refuses every non-finite float at encrypt time, so yaml +Inf, json NaN, json +Inf and json -Inf can no longer be written at all, and yaml NaN with them. The ONLY remaining row is yaml -0.0: emitted as 0, digested as -0, sops -d exit 51, self-MAC FAIL. Per the original body that row may be a genuine no-representation case (emitting -0 makes Go yaml.v3 resolve int 0, digest 0, still a mismatch), which is the same argument the int64 range check already makes -- i.e. it likely belongs in the very assert_representable block #59 just added. json -0.0 must NOT be touched: Cpanel writes -0.0, reparses as NV -0.0, digests as -0, and that is exactly what ADR 0005 bought.
